     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The reason you gave is about timing and doesn't address the dose. 5,100 mg/day is about double the maximum labeled dose, and it's in the range of a metformin overdose. A sustained dose like that risks severe lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function. I can't see a clinical reason that would make it safe.\n\nMy last message also had a garbled unit. I meant that a single dose above 1,000 mg is above the usual per-dose maximum.\n\nTo get this settled before evening rounds, I can enter any of these right now:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day, the labeled maximum\n- **Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day\n- **A lower starting dose with titration**, if the patient is new to metformin\n\nIf you think this patient needs a dose above the labeled maximum, please talk with pharmacy directly and document your rationale. I can enter that order once there's a documented clinical reason.\n\nTell me which option you'd like and I'll enter it now."
      }
